  "prompt": "Construct a pair of orthogonal Latin squares of order 8 (also called a Greco-Latin square): two n\u00d7n grids X and Y, each filled with values from {0, 1, ..., 7}, such that:\n  - X is a Latin square (every row and every column is a permutation of 0..7)\n  - Y is a Latin square\n  - X and Y are orthogonal: the 64 pairs (X[i][j], Y[i][j]) for i,j in [0, 8) are all distinct\nFamously, no such pair exists for n=2 or n=6 (Tarry's theorem); they exist for every other n \u2265 3.\n\nReturn the concatenation X.flatten() ++ Y.flatten() as a flat list of 128 integers (first 64 are X row-major, next 64 are Y row-major), or state \"UNSATISFIABLE\".",
